    <dialog id="checkin-dialog" aria-labelledby="checkin-title"><div class="dialog-header"><h2 id="checkin-title">Let your people know.</h2><button class="icon-button" data-close aria-label="Close check-in"><svg class="icon"><use href="#i-close"/></svg></button></div><form id="checkin-form"><label>Venue name<input name="venue" maxlength="150" required placeholder="Where are you meeting?" /></label><label class="space-top">Area<input name="area" maxlength="100" placeholder="City or neighborhood" /></label><p class="muted small">This saves a private check-in for two hours. Share it only with people you choose. A sent message can’t be recalled, and its copy will not expire.</p><div class="form-actions"><button class="button button-outline" type="button" data-action="clear-checkin">Clear check-in</button><button class="button button-peach" type="submit">Save check-in</button></div><button class="text-button full-width space-top" type="button" data-action="share-checkin">Share the saved check-in <span>↗</span></button></form></dialog>
    <dialog id="ride-dialog" aria-labelledby="ride-title"><div class="dialog-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">GET A SOBER RIDE</p><h2 id="ride-title">Make the next stop easy.</h2></div><button class="icon-button" data-close aria-label="Close ride planner"><svg class="icon"><use href="#i-close"/></svg></button></div><p class="muted">Choose a destination, then confirm the pickup, fare, and ride inside the provider’s app. Afterglow never claims you are safe to drive.</p><form id="ride-book-form"><label>Pickup<input name="pickup" maxlength="220" placeholder="Leave blank to use your current location"/></label><label class="space-top">Destination<input name="destination" maxlength="220" required placeholder="Home, hotel, bar, or restaurant address"/></label><div class="ride-links"><button class="button button-peach" type="submit" data-provider="uber">Continue with Uber ↗</button><button class="button button-outline" type="submit" data-provider="lyft">Continue with Lyft ↗</button></div><p class="tiny muted">No ride is booked until you review and confirm it with Uber or Lyft. Availability and destination prefill depend on the provider and device.</p></form><div class="form-divider"></div><form id="ride-form"><label>Save my backup plan<select name="ride" required><option value="">Choose a plan</option><option>Rideshare or taxi</option><option>Sober designated driver</option><option>Public transit with a friend</option><option>Staying overnight</option></select></label><button class="button button-outline full-width space-top" type="submit">Save my ride plan</button></form><button class="text-button full-width space-top" data-action="home-safe">I’ve arrived home safely <span>✓</span></button></dialog>
    <dialog id="guide-dialog" class="wide-dialog" aria-labelledby="guide-title"><div class="dialog-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">GOOD TO KNOW</p><h2 id="guide-title">A little night-out wisdom.</h2></div><button class="icon-button" data-close aria-label="Close guide"><svg class="icon"><use href="#i-close"/></svg></button></div><div class="guide-content"><h3>A beverage isn’t always one standard drink.</h3><p>The U.S. reference is about 14 grams of pure alcohol. We use the volume and strength you enter to calculate that amount. A 12 oz beer at 5%, 5 oz wine at 12%, and 1.5 oz spirit at 40% each contain about one standard drink. <a href="https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/alcohols-effects-health/what-standard-drink" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIAAA reference ↗</a></p><h3>A BAC model is an approximation, not a measurement.</h3><p>The optional estimate converts every serving’s actual volume and ABV into grams of ethanol, then uses Watson’s adult total-body-water equations with height, weight, age, a blood-water factor of 0.8, and the time of each logged drink. The center estimate uses 0.015 percentage points of elimination per hour. The displayed modeled band also tests 0.010–0.025 per hour and, when requested, both adult reference equations. It is an assumption band—not a confidence interval or a measured BAC.</p><p>The model assumes alcohol is absorbed immediately even though absorption can continue after the last drink. Food, medications, individual physiology, body composition, inaccurate pours, and missing entries can change the real value substantially. Water, coffee, and a new tracking session do not clear alcohol. This tool provides no “safe to drive” status or sobriety time. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6986753/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watson et al. ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/risky-driving/drunk-driving" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NHTSA: BAC and driving ↗</a></p><h3>Your cap is your plan.</h3><p>It’s not a medical recommendation or a safe allowance. You can still record drinks above your cap to keep the log honest. Alcohol-free nights are welcome.</p><div class="notice warning"><div><h3>Know when to get help.</h3><p>If someone is hard to wake, has slow or irregular breathing, or has a seizure, call 911 in the U.S. or your local emergency number. Stay with them; don’t wait for a BAC estimate. <a href="https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/understanding-dangers-of-alcohol-overdose" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIAAA overdose guidance ↗</a></p></div></div><h3>Your privacy stays part of the plan.</h3><p>Profiles, measurements, drinks, and plans are stored on this browser only—even when you sign in. Sharing includes only the plan or selected venue shown to you; no body measurements or BAC. A saved check-in expires locally after two hours. Already shared messages remain with their recipients. Stock venue photos come from Unsplash; connected searches and their photos come from Google.</p></div></dialog>
